<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00LK">Notation <span data-tag="00LK">2.4.5.2</span> <span class="named">(Cubes as Simplicial Sets)</span>.</a> Let $I$ be a set. We let $\operatorname{\raise {0.1ex}{\square }}^{I}$ denote the simplicial set given by the product $\prod _{i \in I} \Delta ^1$. We will refer to $\operatorname{\raise {0.1ex}{\square }}^{I}$ as the <em>$I$-cube</em>. Equivalently, we can describe $\operatorname{\raise {0.1ex}{\square }}^{I}$ as the nerve of the power set $P(I) = \{  I_0 \subseteq I \} $, where we regard $P(I)$ as partially ordered with respect to inclusion. </p>
<p>In the special case where $I$ is the set $\{  1, 2, \ldots , n \} $ for some nonnegative integer $n$, we will denote the simplicial set $\operatorname{\raise {0.1ex}{\square }}^{I}$ by $\operatorname{\raise {0.1ex}{\square }}^{n}$ and refer to it as the <em>standard $n$-cube</em>. </p>
</article>
